cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  boundcompress: certifying that a declared per-sample error bound held, and
  reporting what the guarantee cost
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-24
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Compresses a one-dimensional telemetry array under a declared absolute,
  pointwise relative or range-relative per-sample error bound and issues a
  certificate: the measured maximum per-sample error, the index where it
  occurred, the margin to the bound, the number of violations, and the rate in
  actual encoded bytes. The error guarantee lives entirely in the quantiser;
  everything downstream of it -- a closed-loop integer predictor, a zigzag and
  escape symbol mapping, and a static range Asymmetric Numeral Systems coder
  -- is lossless on the integer symbol stream, so swapping the predictor moves
  the rate and cannot move a single reconstructed sample, and two predictors
  are compared at identical rather than matched distortion. The encoder
  verifies every sample against the declared bound at encode time, corrects
  the samples floating-point rounding puts outside it, and refuses to encode
  rather than issue a certificate it cannot support. Validation is level 2.
  The bound held on 1,310,720 individual sample-versus-bound comparisons
  across eight signals, three bound modes, six predictors and eight bounds
  spanning five decades, with a tightest observed margin of 1.543376e-12 and
  zero rounding corrections needed; eleven deliberate violations were caught
  and located, including one of one part in 1e9 of the bound and a
  reconstruction one unit in the last place past it. Rate is always the length
  of a container that round-trips, never an entropy estimate: the first-order
  entropy is reported beside it as a memoryless reference and a coder with
  memory was measured below it on three of eight signals, by a factor of 6.7
  on one. Two results are unfavourable to this package and are published as
  measured. Its own static order-0 entropy coder is beaten by lzma or bz2 on
  six of eight signals at the median swept bound and by a factor of 4.788 on a
  counter channel, so the contribution is the certificate and the cost
  accounting rather than the coder. And a classical integer predictor wins 14
  of 40 benchmark cells: the strongest classical predictor on these signals is
  first-order previous rather than second-order linear, linear wins outright
  on the two piecewise-linear channels, and the learned perceptron wins the
  median cell by 1.3451 per cent while losing one by 55.8824 per cent, costs
  2328 bytes of parameters that take 36.4 arrays to pay back, did not converge
  within its iteration cap, and is not bit-exact across machines because tanh
  is a libm call. The learned ridge predictor has a negative mean saving and
  never pays back its 120 bytes. Its ensemble dispersion, offered as an
  uncertainty output, has a median Spearman rank correlation of 0.0280 with
  the realised residual. The named third-party baselines numcodecs and zfpy
  are not installed in the measured environment and no rate-distortion number
  in this repository is taken from either; the substitution to the Python
  standard library is recorded as a deviation. All numbers are measured
  against a seeded synthetic telemetry generator whose equations are printed
  in its module docstring; no flight data is used anywhere. A flight
  implementation should use a standardised codec such as CCSDS 121.0-B-3 or
  122.0-B-2. Research grade. Not flight-qualified, not certified, not approved
  for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - error-bounded-compression
  - telemetry
  - satellite-communications
  - rate-distortion
  - predictive-coding
  - entropy-coding
  - rans
  - certificate
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Shannon
        given-names: "C. E."
    title: "A mathematical theory of communication"
    journal: "Bell System Technical Journal"
    volume: 27
    issue: 3
    year: 1948
    notes: >-
      The source-coding theorem behind the entropy figures in
      boundcompress.entropy. Reported as a memoryless reference and never as a
      lower bound: a coder with memory was measured below it on three of eight
      signals.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Duda
        given-names: "J."
    title: >-
      Asymmetric numeral systems: entropy coding combining speed of Huffman
      coding with compression rate of arithmetic coding
    year: 2013
    notes: >-
      arXiv:1311.2540. The static rANS coder in boundcompress.rans, in the
      16-bit renormalisation configuration with a 4096-slot frequency table.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Gersho
        given-names: "A."
      - family-names: Gray
        given-names: "R. M."
    title: "Vector Quantization and Signal Compression"
    publisher:
      name: "Kluwer Academic Publishers"
    year: 1992
    notes: >-
      Chapter 4 for the round-to-nearest uniform quantiser error bound that
      mode='abs' rests on, and for the high-rate result that makes a geometric
      bound sweep the right spacing.
  - type: conference-paper
    authors:
      - family-names: Liang
        given-names: "X."
      - family-names: Di
        given-names: "S."
      - family-names: Tao
        given-names: "D."
      - family-names: Li
        given-names: "S."
      - family-names: Li
        given-names: "S."
      - family-names: Guo
        given-names: "H."
      - family-names: Chen
        given-names: "Z."
      - family-names: Cappello
        given-names: "F."
    title: >-
      Error-controlled lossy compression optimized for high compression ratios
      of scientific datasets
    collection-title: "IEEE International Conference on Big Data"
    year: 2018
    notes: >-
      Log-domain quantisation for a pointwise relative error bound, the
      construction reimplemented in boundcompress.quantise for mode='rel'.
      Reimplemented, not imported; no number here is taken from SZ.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Makhoul
        given-names: "J."
    title: "Linear prediction: a tutorial review"
    journal: "Proceedings of the IEEE"
    volume: 63
    issue: 4
    year: 1975
    notes: >-
      The linear-prediction feature set the learned predictor fits, with a
      local-gain normalisation the decoder can reproduce in exact integer
      arithmetic.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Hildebrand
        given-names: "F. B."
    title: "Introduction to Numerical Analysis"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "Dover"
    year: 1987
    notes: >-
      Chapter 2 for the finite-difference extrapolation operators implemented
      as the classical predictors, and for their exactness on polynomial
      sequences of the matching degree.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Wager
        given-names: "S."
      - family-names: Hastie
        given-names: "T."
      - family-names: Efron
        given-names: "B."
    title: >-
      Confidence intervals for random forests: the jackknife and the
      infinitesimal jackknife
    journal: "Journal of Machine Learning Research"
    volume: 15
    year: 2014
    notes: >-
      Why the per-member spread reported by the learned predictor is a
      dispersion measure and not a confidence interval. Its measured median
      Spearman correlation with the realised residual is 0.0280.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Pedregosa
        given-names: "F."
      - family-names: Varoquaux
        given-names: "G."
      - family-names: Gramfort
        given-names: "A."
    title: "Scikit-learn: machine learning in Python"
    journal: "Journal of Machine Learning Research"
    volume: 12
    year: 2011
    notes: >-
      Ridge and MLPRegressor, used to fit the learned predictor. Their
      parameters are then extracted and the forward pass is evaluated by this
      package in elementwise numpy, so that the encoder's batch evaluation and
      the decoder's sequential one agree bit-for-bit.
  - type: standard
    title: "Lossless Data Compression"
    institution:
      name: "Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems"
    number: "CCSDS 121.0-B-3"
    year: 2020
    notes: >-
      The standard a flight implementation should use, and the source of the
      unit-delay preprocessing idea behind the 'previous' predictor. No
      numerical value in this repository is taken from it.
  - type: standard
    title: "Image Data Compression"
    institution:
      name: "Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems"
    number: "CCSDS 122.0-B-2"
    year: 2017
    notes: >-
      Named in the README's alternatives table as the standard for
      two-dimensional spacecraft imagery, which this one-dimensional package
      does not address. No numerical value in this repository is taken from it.
